{
  // THE COMPILER'S CHECK ON THE JAVASCRIPT (github#145). `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.contracts.json`,
  // run by scripts/check-js-contracts.mjs, which scripts/lint.mjs calls -- so a broken annotation
  // in src/page.js or plugin/main.js fails the same gate a lint finding does, and the pre-push
  // hook and release.ps1 inherit it through `npm run lint`.
  //
  // A THIRD CONFIG, FOR THE SAME REASON THERE IS A SECOND. checkJs cannot be per-file, and it
  // cannot go in tsconfig.json: that program is typescript-eslint's, so every diagnostic here
  // would arrive as a lint finding instead, and esbuild reads tsconfig.json BY NAME, so a flag
  // set there can reach a bundle. esbuild does not look for this file, and neither does eslint.
  //
  // WHAT IT WAS WORTH, MEASURED. Before github#145 the shared program checked only
  // src/engine/**/*.ts; the JavaScript carried annotations nothing verified. Running this
  // config's settings over the tree on 2026-09-14 reported 138 diagnostics (136 page, two
  // plugin) across 105 lines, and an in-memory `/** @type {VaultData} */ var DATA = 42` drew
  // zero errors from the whole gate. Both are zero now, and
  // scripts/check-js-contracts.mjs re-injects that probe on every run so the second one
  // cannot quietly go back to being true.
  //
  // strict IS OFF, inherited. Strict null checking over the JavaScript is a separate, later,
  // measured step -- github#55's ratchet -- and turning it on here would bury 138 real
  // annotation defects under several hundred `string | null` ones. strictFunctionTypes is on,
  // inherited from tsconfig.json for the reason stated there.
  //
  // include IS tsconfig.json's, VERBATIM. The point is to check what that program already
  // names, with checkJs on; a different include would make the two disagree about what the
  // file set is, and the first thing to fall through the difference would be a new file.
  //
  // ITS SIBLING BREAKS THAT RULE ON PURPOSE (github#156). tsconfig.contracts-node.json checks
  // src/build-graph.mjs, which is node code and needs `types: ["node"]` -- and that is exactly
  // why it cannot be one program with this one: measured, node types on THIS include accept
  // `process.env` and `Buffer` in src/page.js, which is browser code, and report zero. So this
  // config stays the browser and plugin one with `types: []` inherited, and the node-side entry
  // point is named there rather than here. That file's comment carries the number.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true
  },
  "include": ["plugin/**/*.js", "plugin/**/*.mjs", "plugin/**/*.d.ts", "src/page.js", "src/globals.d.ts", "src/engine/**/*.ts"]
}
